I met with another university of rochester studen to discuss the costs and benefits of the current NEPA enviornmental reveiw system.
We discussed the possibility of shifting the burden of safegaurds away from the legal system and lenghty reports and to a more local level with a local panel of experts who can better connect with the community.
Below is my recording of our conversation and my analysis of some previous works on the topic by the likes of Potter and Doomburg
https://github.com/hchambe6-dot/econ238-portfolio/blob/hchambe6-NEPA-Discussion/IMG_2706.mp3

My Analysis: Both Potter and Doomberg agree that the mechanism is the risk of litigation shaping behavior before any lawsuit has even been filed. Agencies act as though they are already in court before they have even been sued and spend vast amounts of resources defending themselves from even the possibility of legal action. This means more resources going towards lawyers and less towards engineers, leading to less change and growth in all agencies.
	Howard proposes the creation of new institutions to solve the problem of wasted resources, while Doomberg thinks the solution would be to remove the barriers already existing from old institutions.
	In a world where NEPA was fully repealed and institutions could do whatever they liked without the need for governmental environmental overview much progress could be made in some (good or bad) direction. The removal of NEPA would free up resources to spur the boots of progress, IE that money that was going to environmental reports can instead go to a few new solar panels. In this world the lack of regulations would certainly cause at least some environmental harm, and people would find some way to put the system back into check, whether by making more laws or boycotting/ protesting new developments.
	Without these regulations communities are at greater risk of environmental damage, whether it be because of profit incentives or bad faith development, the average joe near new build sites pays the cost. But not all of it is cost, say a new coal plant opens up, sure the air quality in the nearby towns gets worse but… there are new jobs and economic growth, your electricity gets cheaper and you get more opportunities and marginally cheaper goods.
	One thing my discussion partner mentioned that I hadnt thought of was the idea that we should shift the review and environmental impact discussions to more localised and smaller groups. This would allow a greater understanding of the local environment, have more input from experts and cheapen the man hours necessary. This in combination with a higher burden to convict and sue companies would lessen the costs while hopefully keeping the same level of safeguards.
